# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# gramps-live-api: keep personal data out of a public repository.
#
# This is a *public* repository owned by a private person. Nothing below may
# ever be committed. The PII guard (src/gramps_live_api/core/pii_guard.py) is
# the enforcement; this block is the first line of defence.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Genealogy artefacts -- family tree exports in any of their many formats.
*.ged
*.gedcom
*.gramps
*.gpkg
*.grdb
*.ftm
*.paf
*.gno
*.gw

# Database files -- a Gramps tree on disk is one of these.
*.db
*.db3
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite-journal
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm
*.bsddb

# Media -- scans, photographs and documents attached to people.
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.png
*.gif
*.tif
*.tiff
*.bmp
*.heic
*.pdf
*.mp3
*.mp4
*.mov
*.wav

# Anything named like a credential.
*token*
*secret*
*credential*
.env
.env.*

# ...but every pattern above is matched against every path, and source files
# are routinely named after the thing they handle: a test module called
# test_..._credentials.py matches *credential*. Nothing warns you -- the file
# is simply absent from the clone CI builds, and the suite that passes there is
# quietly smaller than the one that passed locally. Never hide the source trees.
# Enforced by tests/integration/test_repository_hygiene.py.
!src/**/*.py
!tests/**/*.py
!.github/**/*.yml
!*.md

# Local workflow scaffolding. The prompt files here are deployed from another
# repository and regenerated by a script that names its own source, so a copy
# committed here is a fork that goes stale -- and prompt files are where scan
# output, dispositions and local paths collect. It was neither tracked nor
# ignored (#27): governed by nothing, one ordinary `git add` from publication.
#
# Deliberately AFTER the negations above. This directory holds only markdown
# and `!*.md` is unanchored, so it is exactly the shape that negation re-admits.
# Measured both orders: a negation cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, so either works -- last-match-wins costs nothing and
# removes the question. No leading slash, for the reason the mkdocs entry gives.
# Enforced by tests/integration/test_repository_hygiene.py.
.claude/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS BLOCK MUST STAY LAST IN THE FILE.
#
# The local-only personal deny-list: the one file in this project that holds
# real names. Never committed. See CONTRIBUTING.md for how to create one.
#
# Git applies the LAST matching rule, so a negation written after this one
# re-includes the variant it matches. That is not hypothetical: the four
# negations above silently re-admitted .pii-denylist.md, src/.pii-denylist.py,
# tests/.pii-denylist.py and .github/.pii-denylist.yml -- a backup full of
# personal literals, stageable with an ordinary `git add`.
#
# Verify with the exit code of `git check-ignore -q`, never by reading this
# file and never from `check-ignore -v` output, which prints the matching line
# even when that line is a negation. Enforced by
# tests/integration/test_pii_guard_over_a_tree.py.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.pii-denylist*
